orical drama about a medieval Welsh prince leading a rebellion against Engl=
ish rule in the 15th century. Stay with me.</span></p><p style=3D"font:norm=
al 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The production=
 was unlike any other celebration of a national story I=E2=80=99ve seen bef=
ore.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;m=
argin:0 0 15px"><span>It wasn=E2=80=99t =E2=80=9CBraveheart,=E2=80=9D where=
 legend has it that the 14th-century Scottish war hero William Wallace crie=
d =E2=80=9CFreedom!=E2=80=9D just before being executed =E2=80=94 but ultim=
ately inspired his countrymen to win their independence from England.</span=
></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 =
15px"><span>And it wasn=E2=80=99t =E2=80=9CHamilton,=E2=80=9D where America=
=E2=80=99s founding fathers won </span><span style=3D"font-style:italic">th=
eir</span><span> independence from England and wrote the most ambitious con=
stitution in history (while rapping about it).</span></p><p style=3D"font:n=
ormal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The Welsh n=
ever did regain their independence from England. But any nation-building ex=
ercise worth its salt needs to land on a note of triumph somehow. And so =
=E2=80=9COwain & Henry=E2=80=9D instead chose to celebrate values like root=
s, community, mercy =E2=80=94 and holding on to a strong national identity.=
 As Prince Owain Glyndwr, played by the great Michael Sheen, puts it: =E2=
=80=9CWe are still here.=E2=80=9D It was a reminder that the human instinct=
 to find meaning and comfort in our national identities, then as now, can t=
ake many forms.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;c=
olor:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>(I will say it was long. Over three hours.=
